text:
Back the blind system — but do it proactively, not as a sulk or a confession.

The countersign proposal is weak because it leaves you still making the lists, with a special exception for staff children. That keeps the optics of insider influence and probably makes parents more suspicious, not less. The head’s suggestion may be meant kindly, but it will look like a protective fudge. Pushing back against both is the worst option: it will read as defending a privilege, even if you inherited the practice and did it openly.

The strongest practical position is to say, plainly, that you no longer think the front office should control pupil placement, especially where staff children are concerned. You can support the governor’s proposal while asking for it to be properly designed rather than punitive. For example:

- class lists should be produced by teachers/head using agreed criteria;
- the office provides administrative support only;
- staff children are allocated through the same process with no parental or staff input;
- the policy is written down and published;
- there is no retrospective blame attached to you.

Before the meeting, speak privately to the head. Say something like: “I know your proposal was meant to protect me, but I don’t think countersigning will restore confidence. The cleanest answer is to remove my discretion entirely. I don’t want my child, or any staff child, to benefit from my role.” That protects your relationship with the head while showing integrity.

At the vote, do not argue that “everyone did it before”. That defence will land badly. You can acknowledge the old practice without endorsing it: “I followed what had been done previously, but I accept that the perception of favouritism is damaging, and I support a process that removes that risk.”

If your daughter does not get the popular teacher next year, that is not a punishment. It is the cost of being part of a fair system — and it will be much better for her, and for you, than being known as the child who got the preferred class because her parent worked in the office.
